{"title":"2004 AIME I Problems/Problem 8","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Problem\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Solution_1\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 1</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Solution_2\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 2</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#Solution_3\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 3</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-5\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">5</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-6\"><a href=\"#See_also\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">6</span> <span class=\"toctext\">See also</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2004_AIME_I_Problems/Problem_8&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Define a regular <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" />-pointed star to be the union of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> line segments <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/f/01f4f200f47795647a9a805fac42748b9be3c75f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P_1P_2, P_2P_3,\\ldots, P_nP_1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"162\" height=\"16\" /> such that\n</p>\n<ul><li>the points <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/b/2/db2871775f95e5aa1d50049c62cb4d81bb1186fa.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P_1, P_2,\\ldots, P_n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"106\" height=\"16\" /> are coplanar and no three of them are collinear,</li>\n<li>each of the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> line segments intersects at least one of the other line segments at a point other than an endpoint,</li>\n<li>all of the angles at <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/b/2/db2871775f95e5aa1d50049c62cb4d81bb1186fa.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P_1, P_2,\\ldots, P_n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"106\" height=\"16\" /> are congruent,</li>\n<li>all of the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> line segments <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/f/01f4f200f47795647a9a805fac42748b9be3c75f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P_1P_2, P_2P_3,\\ldots, P_nP_1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"162\" height=\"16\" /> are congruent, and</li>\n<li>the path <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/f/01f4f200f47795647a9a805fac42748b9be3c75f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P_1P_2, P_2P_3,\\ldots, P_nP_1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"162\" height=\"16\" /> turns counterclockwise at an angle of less than 180 degrees at each vertex.</li></ul>\n<p>There are no regular 3-pointed, 4-pointed, or 6-pointed stars. All regular 5-pointed stars are similar, but there are two non-similar regular 7-pointed stars. How many non-similar regular 1000-pointed stars are there?\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_1\">Solution 1</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2004_AIME_I_Problems/Problem_8&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 1\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<hr />\n<p>We use the <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Principle_of_Inclusion-Exclusion\" title=\"Principle of Inclusion-Exclusion\">Principle of Inclusion-Exclusion</a> (PIE).\n</p><p>If we join the adjacent vertices of the  regular <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" />-star, we get a regular <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" />-gon. We number the vertices of this <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" />-gon in a counterclockwise direction: <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/4/3/9436ede40e6b15d9c9d160e4b3d50ec2a24c9a02.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0, 1, 2, 3, \\ldots, n-1.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"146\" height=\"16\" />\n</p><p>A regular <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" />-star will be formed if we choose a vertex number <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/5/0/f5047d1e0cbb50ec208923a22cd517c55100fa7b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"10\" />, where <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/6/5/6652338fa86056042eeea8e1fa9922d22b87740d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0 \\le m \\le n-1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"114\" height=\"15\" />, and then form the line segments by joining the following pairs of vertex numbers:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/f/b/0fb926f058716cf622946474887aadd51765c399.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(0 \\mod{n}, m \\mod{n}),$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"179\" height=\"18\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/b/41b8854760050a12729a7413dd09fed63c18f5fd.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(m \\mod{n}, 2m \\mod{n}),$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"195\" height=\"18\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/b/9/9b98901cf180ec19628e968423b31840e62db170.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(2m \\mod{n}, 3m \\mod{n}),$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"204\" height=\"18\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/c/b/9cb5258eafafda9cea1644e873ab37c1779c229e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\cdots,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"31\" height=\"9\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/9/4/79451f765516725d464b839837770c245b396780.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$((n-2)m \\mod{n}, (n-1)m \\mod{n}),$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"299\" height=\"18\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/c/2/5c2ffdcd41f52dea5cd5b71b9d4160c057c44c43.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$((n-1)m \\mod{n}, 0 \\mod{n}).$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"235\" height=\"18\" />\n</p><p>If <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/e/3/be326359a42fc44e7b1483cabfad233f457b44d9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\gcd(m,n) &gt; 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"108\" height=\"18\" />, then the star degenerates into a regular <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/2/3/123c784fe28471df5d2f9c0c658e77eabe2a03b9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{n}{\\gcd(m,n)}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -17px\" width=\"78\" height=\"37\" />-gon or a (2-vertex) line segment if\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/f/8cf17d0e9c8c9efa277923118afdcb9df9642295.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{n}{\\gcd(m,n)}= 2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -17px\" width=\"113\" height=\"37\" />. Therefore, we need to find all <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/5/0/f5047d1e0cbb50ec208923a22cd517c55100fa7b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"10\" /> such that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/a/c7a7986370c73f39e8e4cc79eaa379bac4e14eec.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\gcd(m,n) = 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"108\" height=\"18\" />.\n</p><p>Note that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/8/2/6828e19ac34baac0fbf8cc19591231106eb408d0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n = 1000 = 2^{3}5^{3}.$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"132\" height=\"15\" />\n</p><p>Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/4/d/c4d5d71260f6568a73acbf7b19e0cbae624161d2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$S = \\{1,2,3,\\ldots, 1000\\}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"173\" height=\"18\" />, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/4/6/14677f7b49b27b7d99d69f2e805c349b4f313372.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A_{i}= \\{i \\in S \\mid i\\, \\textrm{ divides }\\,1000\\}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"226\" height=\"18\" />. The number of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/5/0/f5047d1e0cbb50ec208923a22cd517c55100fa7b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"10\" />'s that are not relatively prime to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/e/e/6ee927e1332358c96c62c277441c907c4f51057f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1000$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"38\" height=\"14\" /> is:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/1/8/71884aceaa2180bacc4452034e66168d1331bb73.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mid A_{2}\\cup A_{5}\\mid = \\mid A_{2}\\mid+\\mid A_{5}\\mid-\\mid A_{2}\\cap A_{5}\\mid$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"308\" height=\"18\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/8/0/080ca0610f21e94f0b3ee90875e697f05c769d1b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$= \\left\\lfloor \\frac{1000}{2}\\right\\rfloor+\\left\\lfloor \\frac{1000}{5}\\right\\rfloor-\\left\\lfloor \\frac{1000}{2 \\cdot 5}\\right\\rfloor$\" style=\"vertical-align: -17px\" width=\"242\" height=\"43\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/3/a/03a4fef3eed76a5a4822372ca6c3c342c57404b9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$= 500+200-100 = 600.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"200\" height=\"14\" />\n</p><p>Vertex numbers <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/8/d/38dae5317b62aa197f0f45b8ab9c047de015a26a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n-1=999$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"93\" height=\"13\" /> must be excluded as values for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/5/0/f5047d1e0cbb50ec208923a22cd517c55100fa7b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"10\" /> since otherwise a regular n-gon, instead of an n-star, is formed.\n</p><p>The cases of a 1st line segment of (0, m) and (0, n-m) give the same star. Therefore we should halve the count to get non-similar stars.\n</p><p>Therefore, the number of non-similar 1000 pointed stars is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/6/b/e6bb6a7dd537db1656cb7681f86d5adc666b9cca.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{1000-600-2}{2}= \\boxed{199}.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"187\" height=\"37\" />\n</p>\n<hr />\n<p>Note that in general, the number of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" />-pointed stars is given by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/2/0/d209c6af01d777f3dd6f280849a49f532beb89ec.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{\\phi(n)}{2} - 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"70\" height=\"38\" /> (dividing by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> to remove the reflectional symmetry, subtracting <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> to get rid of the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />-step case), where <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/d/a/edae70ddedf89e790936ed4035c5ca1480212e0f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\phi(n)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"34\" height=\"18\" /> is the <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Euler%27s_totient_function\" title=\"Euler&#39;s totient function\">Euler's totient function</a>. It is well-known that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/e/9/3e98fc9ac624ccb30f1ff80193f6129930d091e7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\phi(n) = n\\left(1-\\frac{1}{p_1}\\right)\\left(1-\\frac{1}{p_2}\\right)\\cdots \\left(1-\\frac{1}{p_n}\\right)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -17px\" width=\"336\" height=\"43\" />, where <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/b/8/eb8506ce783b731e9522ecbf794eeb265d74badd.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p_1,\\,p_2,\\ldots,\\,p_n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"105\" height=\"11\" /> are the distinct prime factors of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" />. Thus <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/d/e/fde1b305416f4ea5153d9c6398d21c215d386cbc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\phi(1000) = 1000\\left(1 - \\frac 12\\right)\\left(1 - \\frac 15\\right) = 400$\" style=\"vertical-align: -17px\" width=\"320\" height=\"43\" />, and the answer is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/c/2/3c2c0592cc9c674984771a05d8c5dc62d4c5c501.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{400}{2} - 1 = 199$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"113\" height=\"37\" />.\n</p>\n<hr />\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_2\">Solution 2</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2004_AIME_I_Problems/Problem_8&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 2\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Continue as before by cyclically constructing a star by taking every <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c325612684d41304b9751c175df7bcc0f61f64f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"14\" />-th point. We can think of this construction as the orbit of a point by a rotation <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/4/2/8427ffcaaae740014d1acbf5bd90a28ca2a7e841.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$R^k\\in D_{1000}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"89\" height=\"20\" />. Then, we want <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/b/e/6beb0f1f9ce32667c224690a7f101c35880889b2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$|R^k|=1000$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"95\" height=\"22\" />, meaning that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/2/a/c2a7c7ab9e8270214d757a95cb41143e8c62e588.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k\\in (\\mathbb Z/1000\\mathbb Z)^\\times$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"129\" height=\"21\" />. Then, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c325612684d41304b9751c175df7bcc0f61f64f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"14\" /> is coprime to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/e/e/6ee927e1332358c96c62c277441c907c4f51057f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1000$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"38\" height=\"14\" />, meaning <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c325612684d41304b9751c175df7bcc0f61f64f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"14\" /> may take on <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/5/8/9586664416fa747e3635a641b415a481edc007aa.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\phi(1000)=400$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"112\" height=\"18\" /> possible values. For any <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/6/c/d6cbce408bd96b8a123ed5905b154ecc7443d4f3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$R^k$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"24\" height=\"17\" />, the dihedral symmetry <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/d/0/ad0b2b88ac071837fe9ff83d3b7ceff294f58e24.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$TR^k$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"37\" height=\"17\" /> defines the figure-preserving bijection <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/4/5/d4536f03415bf28163976c4b8f0d43302a1efc8d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$R^k\\mapsto R^{-k}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"85\" height=\"17\" />, meaning half of our possible values of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c325612684d41304b9751c175df7bcc0f61f64f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"14\" /> are similar. Finally, the condition that each edge must intersect another rules out <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/e/d/5ed8470bc717a49aafe4556c7e2c2e11ddc7b687.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k=1$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"42\" height=\"13\" />, giving a total of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/f/7/ef73cdbcb926c5df19e5c878af4dd8c2ccdfcd0e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{400}2-1=\\boxed{199}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"127\" height=\"38\" /> pointed stars.\n</p><p>~pineconee\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_3\">Solution 3</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2004_AIME_I_Problems/Problem_8&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 3\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Consider the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" />-vertex regular polygon. This polygon has exactly <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/b/4/2b4c21f0e5818daeb4d9b3ac0c7e76ccf9056b86.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{n(n-3)}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"69\" height=\"38\" /> diagonals, excluding edges. Such a star is formed in the following manner.\n</p><p>Choose a position point (a point that is fixed, say <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/4/1/241e6e35ad6d73fb2ef98244b0211deb2f25222a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P_0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"17\" height=\"15\" />). Consider a point, and draw the line from <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/4/1/241e6e35ad6d73fb2ef98244b0211deb2f25222a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P_0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"17\" height=\"15\" /> to that new point. Reflect this point over the line of symmetry down from <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/4/1/241e6e35ad6d73fb2ef98244b0211deb2f25222a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P_0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"17\" height=\"15\" />. Now, rotate this figure <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> times to each vertex (obviously, not to the two points in which the segment was first drawn and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/4/1/241e6e35ad6d73fb2ef98244b0211deb2f25222a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P_0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"17\" height=\"15\" />). The result formed should have exactly <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> unique segments that form a unique star. The only way this can be possible is if no common point <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/b/c/cbcefa2a1ec4ea2d90716dba094f67e6cbdfdbee.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P_{\\not \\varphi(n)}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -6px\" width=\"42\" height=\"20\" /> exists such that some mapping will map a diagonal back onto a previous diagonal. Therefore, all such stars must contain segments such that the number of external side lengths between the formed diagonal and its reflection of the polygon is relatively prime to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" />, or <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/3/d/63d7e990c9ebf7adc93ddd233b1239e5e4c0eef3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\varphi(n)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"35\" height=\"18\" /> (the external side lengths are the lengths bounded by the two lines, shown in the figure below, an example of one of the two 7-sided stars.\n</p><p><span typeof=\"mw:File\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=File:2004aimeiq8s3.png\" class=\"mw-file-description\"><img src=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/thumb/5/5c/2004aimeiq8s3.png/550px-2004aimeiq8s3.png\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"550\" height=\"464\" class=\"mw-file-element\" srcset=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/5/5c/2004aimeiq8s3.png 1.5x\" /></a></span>\n</p><p>Now, every star can be reflected over this line of symmetry, and we end up with a star that is also apart of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/3/d/63d7e990c9ebf7adc93ddd233b1239e5e4c0eef3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\varphi(n)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"35\" height=\"18\" />. Therefore, we must divide by 2. (In other terms, we drew a line from <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/4/1/241e6e35ad6d73fb2ef98244b0211deb2f25222a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P_0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"17\" height=\"15\" /> to another point <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/9/5/39544a3f15ea893fc211dc639740fe09d77aef5e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P_{a}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"22\" height=\"17\" />, and we do the same for the point <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/5/0154ec27dd99128fe58ff9389491ab2e3a88022d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P&#39;_{a}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"22\" height=\"20\" /> reflected over the line of symmetry through <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/4/1/241e6e35ad6d73fb2ef98244b0211deb2f25222a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P_0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"17\" height=\"15\" />. That result is the same star overcounted double). So, we have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/0/1/9012dc28b129528fa6bdf2363422cb8bfa398cee.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{\\varphi(n)}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"43\" height=\"40\" />. Are we done? \n</p><p>No! Notice when we draw the star, we also have that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/b/d/4bdca93962a2beaffc172ca394e04fe05bd74ffd.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n-1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"44\" height=\"15\" /> is relatively prime to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> for all <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" />. However, this star is the polygon itself, and that defies our definition of a star, because each segment must have one intersection such that the exterior angle is less than 180. In the case of a polygon, this is never the case, and we already have one copy remaining when we divide by 2.\n</p><p>One might consider the \"zero pointed star\". However, because 0 is not defined to be relatively prime to any number (you cannot divide by 0), we neglect the case entirely.\n</p><p>Therefore, the total number of stars that can be formed given an <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" />-gon is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/6/1/e618f1114930ed7bc2e9ec46dbd2328272dad8d4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{\\varphi(n)}{2} - 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"71\" height=\"38\" />. Plugging in 1000, we have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/d/2/3d2e8e65444f9715a93e7da7b2505dc45f190458.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{199}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"39\" height=\"23\" />.\n</p><p>~Pinotation\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution\">Video Solution</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2004_AIME_I_Problems/Problem_8&amp;action=edit&amp;section=5\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://youtu.be/c-ImoUDkJSM?si=hrzrWcWp0TcxPXlW\">2004 AIME I #8</a>\n</p><p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://mathproblemsolvingskills.wordpress.com/\">MathProblemSolvingSkills.com</a>\n</p><p><br />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_also\">See also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2004_AIME_I_Problems/Problem_8&amp;action=edit&amp;section=6\" title=\"Edit section: See also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"margin:0.5em auto; font-size:95%; border:1px solid black; width:80%;\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td style=\"background:#ccf;text-align:center;\" colspan=\"3\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2004_AIME_I\" title=\"2004 AIME I\">2004 AIME I</a></b> (<b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2004_AIME_I_Problems\" title=\"2004 AIME I Problems\">Problems</a></b> • <b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2004_AIME_I_Answer_Key\" title=\"2004 AIME I Answer Key\">Answer Key</a></b> • <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"http://www.artofproblemsolving.com/Forum/resources.php?c=182&amp;cid=45&amp;year=2004\">Resources</a>)\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Preceded&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2004_AIME_I_Problems/Problem_7\" title=\"2004 AIME I Problems/Problem 7\">Problem 7</a></b>\n</td>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Followed&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2004_AIME_I_Problems/Problem_9\" title=\"2004 AIME I Problems/Problem 9\">Problem 9</a></b>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"3\" style=\"text-align:center;\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2004_AIME_I_Problems/Problem_1\" title=\"2004 AIME I Problems/Problem 1\">1</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2004_AIME_I_Problems/Problem_2\" title=\"2004 AIME I Problems/Problem 2\">2</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2004_AIME_I_Problems/Problem_3\" title=\"2004 AIME I Problems/Problem 3\">3</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2004_AIME_I_Problems/Problem_4\" title=\"2004 AIME I Problems/Problem 4\">4</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2004_AIME_I_Problems/Problem_5\" title=\"2004 AIME I Problems/Problem 5\">5</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2004_AIME_I_Problems/Problem_6\" title=\"2004 AIME I Problems/Problem 6\">6</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2004_AIME_I_Problems/Problem_7\" title=\"2004 AIME I Problems/Problem 7\">7</a> <b>•</b> <a class=\"mw-selflink selflink\">8</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2004_AIME_I_Problems/Problem_9\" title=\"2004 AIME I Problems/Problem 9\">9</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2004_AIME_I_Problems/Problem_10\" title=\"2004 AIME I Problems/Problem 10\">10</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2004_AIME_I_Problems/Problem_11\" title=\"2004 AIME I Problems/Problem 11\">11</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2004_AIME_I_Problems/Problem_12\" title=\"2004 AIME I Problems/Problem 12\">12</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2004_AIME_I_Problems/Problem_13\" title=\"2004 AIME I Problems/Problem 13\">13</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2004_AIME_I_Problems/Problem_14\" title=\"2004 AIME I Problems/Problem 14\">14</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2004_AIME_I_Problems/Problem_15\" title=\"2004 AIME I Problems/Problem 15\">15</a>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" colspan=\"3\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=AIME_Problems_and_Solutions\" title=\"AIME Problems and Solutions\"> All AIME Problems and Solutions</a></b>\n</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<div class=\"print\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;width:100%;justify-content:space-between;align-items:center\">\n<div>\n<p><span class=\"mw-default-size\" typeof=\"mw:File\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=File:AMC_Logo.png\" class=\"mw-file-description\"><img src=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/d/d1/AMC_Logo.png\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"97\" height=\"84\" class=\"mw-file-element\" /></a></span>\n</p>\n</div>\n<div style=\"flex-grow:1;text-align:right\">\n<p>These problems are copyrighted &#169; 